      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Example: Multilevel Inheritance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Suppose we have 3 classes: A, B, and C. In multilevel inheritance, the class B inherits from A and C inherits from B. Here's how the inheritance looks like:"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Class C inherits from class B which also inherits from class A. This is called multilevel inheritance",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Source Code"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "content": "1#include<iostream>\n2using namespace std;\n3\n4class A {\n5    public:\n6        void function_A() {\n7            cout << \"Function of class A\" << endl;\n8        }\n9};\n10\n11class B: public A {\n12    public:\n13        void function_B() {\n14            cout << \"Function of class B\" << endl;\n15        }\n16};\n17\n18class C: public B {};\n19\n20int main() {\n21    \n22    // object of the class C\n23    C obj;\n24    \n25    // call function of the class B\n26    obj.function_B();\n27    \n28    // call function of the class A\n29    obj.function_A();\n30    \n31    return 0;\n32}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "Function of class B\nFunction of class A"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here, when the class B inherits A, the function of A is now inherited to B. That's why the object of the class C is able to access functions of both classes, A and B, even though it only inherits B."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
